WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.899
Recently, RealLifeLore, Wendover
Productions and I had the opportunity to

00:00:03.899 --> 00:00:08.309
travel to the northernmost city in North
America: Barrow, Alaska. While there is

00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:11.969
plenty of fascinating information
regarding this Arctic frontier town, this

00:00:11.969 --> 00:00:15.900
video is going to take a slightly more
personal approach. Instead of facts and

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.289
figures. I'm going to read an excerpt
from the journal I kept in Barrow, and

00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:21.400
share some first impressions.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:29.700
 

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:51.880
[Wind]
[Low-frequency humming noise]

00:00:53.860 --> 00:01:11.700
[Music]

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:17.180
Barrow, Alaska is beautiful. Perhaps not
in the traditional sense, as aside from a

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:20.720
handful of respectable hotels and
government buildings, the city could only

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.600
be described as gritty, faded, and
utilitarian.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:29.120
But for all its dust, and mud, and near total lack of creature comforts,

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.080
Barrow manages to make you smile.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:35.620
Not without a scarf around your face, of
course, as your lips are liable to crack

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:37.360
from the cold should you attempt it.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:42.160
Barrow is a town utterly without pretense.
It makes no excuses for its rugged

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.080
appearance, nor would you expect it to, as
everyone in town has more important things to do.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.360
So, while it is true that
the town is unapologetically Spartan, for

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.780
it to be any other way would seem
disingenuous.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.940
You wouldn't expect an opera house, or a Botanical Garden,

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.800
or any
other such frivolities from a frontier town,

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.040
and you won't find them here, but
what you do get is something much more special.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:10.520
This frozen town of 5,000 people
sits on the very edge of the Arctic

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:13.260
Ocean,
an expanse largely covered in thick sea

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.660
ice and drifts of snow.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.620
Trudging across
the dirty, gray stretch of sand along the

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:22.120
ocean in layers upon layers of winter
clothing, and looking out over the

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:27.160
endless expanse of white makes you
realize how uniquely unsuited humans are to such an environment.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:30.720
The fact that
polar bears are known to wander along

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:34.840
the icy coast just a stone's throw from
the beach certainly adds to that feeling

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.820
of being in a position of inferiority.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:40.520
As you leave the shoreline and walk along the muddy roads

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.880
of Barrow, you can begin
to piece together a ramshackle history

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:49.060
of the stubborn outpost. Cars and trucks
in varying stages of decay litter the

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:53.170
outskirts and snowdrifts of town. Here, a
work truck, half buried in snow, its

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:57.010
windows shattered, and door hanging
limply from its hinges. There, a ruined

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:00.780
old car, paint nearly stripped bare and
sunk into the bumpers in thick, perpetual

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:04.960
mud. An entire lineage of automobiles,
stretching back to the beginning of

00:03:04.969 --> 00:03:08.629
barrow itself, which coughed their last,
and were left to crumble where they

00:03:08.629 --> 00:03:13.620
stood, defeated by conditions too
unforgiving for all but the most robust utility vehicles.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:17.800
As I sit writing this,
the clock inches towards midnight, and

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:21.920
yet outside the window the town remains
bathed in a dull, blue-grey light.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.579
The trucks pass less frequently, and the
goals have mostly settled for the night,

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.580
but the Sun refuses to relinquish its
grip on the day.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:33.840
During this time of year, week after week of unforgiving sunlight makes blackout shades

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:39.379
a necessity, with which, thankfully, our hotel is equipped.
This harsh day-to-day reality is what

00:03:39.379 --> 00:03:44.090
makes Barrow so beautiful in its own
special way. The juxtaposition, that line

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:48.349
drawn in the sand between man and nature,
a place where, for all our ingenuity,

00:03:48.349 --> 00:03:53.599
nature still gets the best of us. It's
not a place I'd like to stay, it's not a

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.000
place I'll likely visit again, but it's
certainly not a place I'll ever forget.

